@ 37.1.2.4.2 - Proto-Mohawk-Oneida to Oneida
# citation: cedh audmanh, from Julian, Charles (2010), “A History of the Iroquoian Languages”,
# 			University of Manitoba, Winnipeg
	”V:[+ long, -falling tone]CV > V:[+ long]”CV
	”V > ”V:[+ long] / _ʔ
	ʔ > ∅ / ”V:[+ long]_
	∅ > i / {V,t}n_k(ʷ)V
	ths > t͡sh
	hs > sh / _{n,w}
	h > ∅ / _Ch
	h > ∅ / k_{s,t͡s}
	h > ∅ / _{sk,st}
	h > ∅ / {st,t͡st}_
	h > ∅ / t͡s_r // “in pre-pausal forms, see also below”
	{h,ʔ} > ∅ / _R “in post-tonic syllables”
	ʔ > h / _C “in post-tonic syllables”
	r > l
	”V:[+ long, +falling tone] > ”V:[- long -falling tone]
	V:[+ long] > V:[- long] _”C(C)(C)V (“this change happens only in the Ontario dialect”)
	# “In addition, a number of sound changes have applied to words only in the
	# 			pre-pausal position. Most of these changes are characterized by the devoicing of one or
	# 			more segments at the end of a word”
	∅ > e / C_{n,l}V(H)#
	∅ > o / C_wV(H)#
	∅ > i / C_jV(H)#
	V:[+ long, +falling tone]C(C)V(H) > V̊ː:[+falling tone]C̊(C̊)V̊(H̊) / _#
	CV:[+ long, +falling tone] > C̊V̊ː:[+falling tone] / _#
	CVʔ > C̊V̊ʔ̊ / _#
	V:[- long] > V̊ / R_#
	OV:[- long] > O̊V̊ / _#
	jV > ∅ / Ci_(H)#
	ʔ > h / _C#
	R > R̊ / _#
	# “Allophonic changes:”
	t͡s > t͡ʃ / _{(h)i,(h)j}
	t t͡ʃ k(ʷ) > d d͡ʒ ɡ(ʷ) / _{V,R}
	s > ʃ / _(h)j
